T*
(When an engineering component contains intricate internal cavities, bores, counterbores,) Tj
T*
(and passageways, standard orthographic views become cluttered with confusing overlapping) Tj
T*
(dashed hidden lines:) Tj
T*
(* Sectional View: An imaginary cutting plane cuts through the component, the portion) Tj
T*
(between the observer and the plane is removed, revealing the interior cross-sectional) Tj
T*
(geometry directly with solid visible lines.) Tj
T*
(* Cutting Plane: Imaginary slicing plane indicated by Cutting Plane Line \(chain thin) Tj
T*
(line with thick ends and direction arrows showing line of sight labeled A-A\).) Tj
T*
(* Section Lining / Hatching: The solid material sliced by the cutting plane is) Tj
T*
(highlighted with thin, continuous parallel hatching lines.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 4.2 Standard Hatching Rules and Conventions \(BIS SP 46\)) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Angle & Spacing: Drawn at 45 degrees to the main baseline of the view \(or 30/60 deg) Tj
T*
(if 45 deg parallels an object edge\). Spacing uniform between 1.5 mm and 3.0 mm) Tj
T*
(depending on view scale.) Tj
T*
(* Adjacent Components in Assembly: Hatched in opposite directions \(e.g., 45 deg left) Tj
T*
(vs 45 deg right\) or with differing line pitches to clearly distinguish distinct) Tj
T*
(mating parts.) Tj
T*
(* Non-Sectioned Standard Elements \(Parts NOT Hatched along their axis\):) Tj
T*
(- Shafts, bolts, nuts, studs, screws, rivets, keys, cotters, pins.) Tj
T*
(- Spokes and arms of wheels, gear teeth, ball and roller bearings.) Tj
T*
(- Thin webs, ribs, and stiffener plates \(when cut longitudinally parallel to their) Tj
T*
(plane, they are left unhatched to avoid giving a false impression of solid thickness\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
